## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical Azure storage patterns, including idempotency design, per-tenant isolation, and integration with core Azure services—essential skills for building enterprise-ready serverless applications.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Functions HTTP trigger for API endpoints
- Azure Table Storage partitioning strategy using tenantId as PartitionKey and fileName as RowKey for efficient, tenant-isolated queries
- ADLS Gen2 Blob Storage for scalable file persistence
- Idempotent design patterns using conditional inserts or upserts in Table Storage
- Retriable operations through transactional writes or try-catch with appropriate retry logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: HTTP-triggered function
module.exports = async function(context, req) {
  const { tenantId, fileName, contentBase64 } = req.body;
  const tableClient = /* Table Storage client */;
  const blobClient = /* ADLS Gen2 blob client */;

  const existing = await tableClient.getEntity(tenantId, fileName);
  if (existing) {
    return { status: 200, body: { message: 'File already uploaded', ...existing } };
  }

  const content = Buffer.from(contentBase64, 'base64');
  await blobClient.upload(content);
  await tableClient.upsert({ partitionKey: tenantId, rowKey: fileName, ... });

  return { status: 201, body: { message: 'File uploaded successfully' } };
}
```

